\section{Introduction}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/figure1.pdf}
\caption{Why escalation needs a guarantee. (a) The team costs up to 6.3 times a single reasoning agent;
(b) some tasks the team solves are already solved by the direct answer $L_0$ (fill) or by $L_0$ or a
single agent $L_1$ (outline); (c) confidence only partly separates right from wrong, and on three settings ranks them backwards; (d) a fixed threshold
that spends as much on average as \climb{} exceeds the tolerance on some settings, while \climb{} stays within $\eps = 5\%$ (replay).}
\label{fig:motivation}
\end{figure*}

Multi-agent systems built from large language models divide a task among roles: a planner decomposes it,
an executor solves it and a reviewer critiques the result over several rounds
\citep{du2024debate,wu2024autogen,hong2024metagpt}. Such pipelines are expensive and not always better. On
the three open models we study, a plan--execute--review pipeline spends 5.4k to 175k tokens per task, 1.3
to 6.3 times what a single agent spends when it simply reasons about the task once
(Figure~\ref{fig:motivation}a), yet some tasks it solves are already solved by a direct answer or a single
agent (Figure~\ref{fig:motivation}b). The team can also make things worse: on some tasks a direct answer or a single agent is right and the
team is wrong, mostly because its first draft is already wrong and its reviewer does not correct it, and
almost half of its losses against the direct answer recur when the team is run again (\S\ref{sec:analysis}). The
question is therefore less whether collaboration helps on average than \emph{when it is worth its cost}
for a given input.

A natural saving is to call the team only when a cheaper configuration is unlikely to suffice; the
difficulty is the rule that decides when. At inference time the system sees only weak proxies for the quality of a cheap answer: the mean
token log-probability of a direct answer separates right from wrong with an AUROC of 0.22 to 0.81, below
chance where greedy decoding produces confident repetitions (Figure~\ref{fig:motivation}c).
Such proxies can work on average but carry no guarantee. A fixed confidence threshold that spends as much on average as our method loses more than 2 points of accuracy on seven of our twelve settings and up to 16 (Figure~\ref{fig:motivation}d), and always stopping at the single agent loses up to 9. Without a bound on the accuracy given up, such savings are hard to
trust, and a practitioner may well keep paying for the full pipeline.

We ask instead how much accuracy a user is willing to give up, and spend as little as possible within
that. A task climbs a ladder whose rungs are cheaper configurations of the same model, a direct answer $L_0$ and then a single
agent $L_1$ at the pipeline's own reasoning setting, and stops at one of them once the evidence suffices, escalating to
the full pipeline otherwise; the system minimises expected cost under a bound on the accuracy lost
relative to always running the pipeline. Because skipping the team can also keep correct answers the team
would miss, this loss is \emph{signed}. When escalation helps less the more confident the cheap answer is, the
optimal rule is a confidence threshold, and the safe thresholds are the most conservative ones.
\climb{} uses this order without assuming the condition: following Learn-then-Test
\citep{angelopoulos2021ltt}, it tests the thresholds from conservative to aggressive with a betting test on the signed loss
\citep{waudbysmith2024betting}, which gives a distribution-free guarantee: with probability at least $1-\delta$, the deployed
policy loses at most $\eps$ accuracy against the team. It answers cheaply where the guarantee allows and runs the pipeline elsewhere,
including on every task when no cheaper policy can be certified, so a weak or misleading signal tends to
cost savings rather than accuracy.

Across three open models and four benchmarks, \climb{} stayed within its tolerance in over 96\% of i.i.d.\ calibration draws, where the guarantee requires 90\%, while saving 38\% of the pipeline's tokens on average at a
5-point tolerance and 65\% at 10 points; where its signal was misleading it saved little rather than losing
accuracy. How the loss is tested can matter as much as the signal: applying the
Hoeffding--Bentkus bound of \citet{wynn2026corrupted} certifies nothing cheaper than the pipeline on the same records, and
clipping the loss at zero certifies less on most settings. Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}
    \setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}
    \setlength{\parskip}{0pt}
    \setlength{\parsep}{0pt}
    \item We measure what a plan--execute--review team adds over a single agent, including the correct
    answers it loses and where those losses arise, and cast the decision of when to call it as a
    constrained problem with a signed loss, under which one monotone-gain condition makes a threshold optimal and testing in order lossless (\S\ref{sec:motivation}, \S\ref{sec:structure}).
    \item We introduce \climb{}, a training-free procedure that escalates on confidence and on the
    agreement of two cheap rungs, and certifies the accuracy lost relative to the team with a
    variance-adaptive betting test inside fixed-sequence testing (\S\ref{sec:calib}).
    \item On three models and four benchmarks we verify that the guarantee holds in practice, show that uncertified rules of
    similar cost can lose unpredictable amounts, and find that, when most losses are zero, variance adaptivity matters as much as keeping the loss's sign (\S\ref{sec:results}).
\end{itemize}
